      'Ask most people how self-control works and they will describe a fight: temptation on one side, willpower on the other, and exhaustion by evening. It is a tidy story, and decades of psychology once backed it. The trouble is, it does not match what highly self-disciplined people actually do.',
      'Reporting on research by psychologists Denise de Ridder and Johanna Peetz, Scientific American describes a quieter picture: people who score high on trait self-control rarely grit their teeth through temptation. They restructure their days so temptation barely appears. The snack never enters the house. The phone charges outside the bedroom. The study session happens at the same desk at the same hour. What looks like iron discipline from the outside is mostly good interior design.',
