      'You locked the front door this morning without a shred of memory about doing it. That is your habit system at work: a piece of behaviour so practised it runs on autopilot, freeing your conscious attention for everything else. BBC Science Focus’s guide to this machinery explains why the autopilot is a superpower — and why hijacking it beats wrestling with it.',
      '## The habit switchboard',
      'Neuroscience points to the infralimbic cortex — a strip of prefrontal real estate sometimes called the habit switchboard. Early in learning, behaviour is deliberate: slow, effortful, conscious. With repetition, control is handed off to this deeper circuitry, and the behaviour begins to run itself, triggered by cues rather than choices. This hand-off is why willpower is the wrong tool for old habits: you are not fighting a decision, you are interrupting a reflex.',
