      'Ask a trusted colleague or friend a specific question: ‘Did I listen well today — what did I do that showed it?’ General questions get polite answers; specific ones get truth. Track what you hear over a few weeks. Like any skill, listening improves fastest when the feedback loop is short and honest.',
      '## Where it usually breaks down',
      'Watch for two failure patterns in yourself. The first is the **fix-it reflex**: the moment a problem appears, your mind starts drafting solutions while the speaker is still talking — and the draft drowns out the rest of their story. The second is **comparative listening**: ‘that happened to me too!’ feels like bonding, but it quietly recentres the conversation on you. Both are kind impulses with rude outcomes. The repair in each case is the same: return to the speaker (‘so how did that leave you?’) and postpone your material until it is genuinely asked for.',
