    excerpt:
      'Why listening is a business imperative: most workplace breakdowns stem from misunderstanding, and pausing to clarify prevents spirals.',
            body: [
      'In leadership settings, listening is often treated as politeness — something nice people do while the real work happens elsewhere. UC Berkeley Executive Education’s deep dive on the art of active listening argues the opposite case: for leaders, listening is not soft. It is the primary instrument for gathering reality.',
      '## Presence is the whole technique',
      'The article’s starting point is presence — being in the room you are in. Leaders are famously in three meetings at once: the one in the calendar, the one on the phone, the one in tomorrow’s board deck. Presence is visible in the first three seconds: device away, body turned, attention actually landing. People report almost instantly whether a leader is with them or merely near them.',
      '## Reflect to check reality',
      'Paraphrasing does double duty for a leader: it makes the speaker feel heard, and it tests whether you have understood. ‘So the timeline is the risk, not the budget — is that right?’ catches more errors per minute than any dashboard. The best operators treat their own first interpretation as a hypothesis.',
      '## Listening through conflict',
      'When stakes rise, listening degrades first — which is exactly when it is worth protecting. The piece recommends listening longest to the position you find least comfortable, and asking questions whose answers you genuinely do not know. Curiosity under pressure is a trainable leadership behaviour, and it is contagious down the hierarchy.',
      '## The strategic payoff',
      'What leaders actually need — the unfiltered signal about customers, morale, and risk — travels almost exclusively through conversations where someone felt safe enough to speak plainly. Active listening is how that safety is built, one exchange at a time. The leader who listens well learns first; the leader who learns first, decides best.',
      '## The question behind the question',
      'Senior leaders hear polished messages all day — reports shaped to be safe to say. Underneath each one is usually a second, unpolished message about risk, morale, or confusion. Active listening is how leaders dig toward it: not by interrogating, but by making candour cheap. ‘What would you say if there were no consequences?’ asked sincerely and answered without flinching, is worth more than any survey. The listening leader’s real product is an organisation that keeps telling them the truth while it is still fixable.',
      'A practical rule to steal for your next week of meetings: in every conversation, make your first contribution a question, and your second a reflection. The information you gather will change what you decide — and, quietly, how people experience being led by you.',
    ],
