    id: 'lab-multitasking-lie',
    slug: 'multitasking-is-a-lie',
    title: 'Multitasking is a lie your brain tells you',
    kind: 'blog',
    source: 'Uneek Learning Lab',
    date: '2023-08-19',
    minutes: 4,
    courseSlug: 'psychology',
    tags: ['attention', 'cognitive load', 'focus'],
    excerpt:
      'You are not doing two things at once; you are switching fast and paying the tax every time. The tax is bigger than you think.',
            body: [
      'The laptop has nine tabs open, a document mid-sentence, a chat window blinking, and a phone face-up within thumb’s reach. It feels like working at full capacity. Neuroscience has a blunter description of the scene: you are task-switching, paying a tax on every transition, and the brain is invoicing you quietly.',
      '## One channel, many queues',
      'The brain has never multitasked in the way the word promises. What it does is **rapidly alternate** attention between tasks — and for anything requiring thought (writing, calculating, composing), the alternation is serial: one fully, then the other. The famous ‘multitasking’ that genuinely runs in parallel is limited to automatic actions (walking while talking). Everything else is switching, and switching is not free.',
      '## The switching tax',
      'Research on attention — including Gloria Mark’s field studies of knowledge workers and lab work on task-switching — puts the cost at a visible slice of time to reorient plus a subtler residue: part of your attention stays stuck on the previous task. Sophie Leroy’s work on **attention residue** showed that after an interruption, performance on the next task sags until attention fully disengages — and it disengages slowly, especially if the interrupted task felt urgent.',
      '## The heavy-media finding',
      'A Stanford study by Clifford Nass and colleagues compared heavy media multitaskers with light ones, expecting the heavy group to be the elite attention athletes. The opposite: heavy multitaskers were **worse** at filtering irrelevant information, worse at managing working memory, and more easily lured by distraction. Habitual switching did not train focus — it trained distraction.',
      '## The quiet cost',
      'Beyond efficiency, there is experience: a mind alternating every ninety seconds rarely reaches the depth where work becomes satisfying. The feeling of busyness substitutes for the feeling of progress. Stress rises because nothing ever quite finishes. The inbox is cleared; the real work is untouched.',
      '## Single-tasking as a competitive advantage',
      '## The myth of the multitasking skill',
      'You will meet people — often your younger self — who insist they are simply built to multitask. The lab evidence is unromantic: what looks like parallel processing is high-frequency switching plus a rising error rate plus a subjectively invisible cost. Heavy multitaskers consistently rate their own performance higher while scoring lower — the tax is hardest to feel in the person paying it. If a job advert asks for a ‘multitasker’, read it as ‘someone who will switch tasks constantly and be blamed for the fragments’. Ask instead what the priorities are — that single question is itself a professional skill.',
      'The fix is old-fashioned and almost embarrassingly effective. Time-box one task, full-screen the window, phone in another room (not face-down — **another room**), and let messages wait their turn. Batch the shallow work into fixed windows so it stops colonising the deep ones. Expect the first twenty minutes to feel slow and restless — that is the detox, not the failure. On the far side of it, most people discover they can do the morning’s real work before lunch — and that the person who appears busiest and the person who is actually productive are rarely the same person.',
    ],
